glimt: make the OpenAPI annotations enforceable

Task 312, plus task 328 raised out of it.

All twelve endpoints were already annotated, because the blocks were written as each
handler landed. So the sweep this task describes would have found nothing, and the
criterion with any value is the last one -- that no endpoint added by PRD 019 is missing
annotations -- which is a claim about the future that only a test can hold.

swaggo annotations are comments. Nothing compiles them, nothing runs them, and a handler
registered without them behaves identically, which makes them the one kind of
documentation that goes stale with no symptom at all. glimtopenapi_test.go parses
routes.go for what is actually registered and cross-checks each handler's doc comment, so
adding a route is what fails rather than forgetting to update a checklist.

The core of it compares the documented @Failure codes against the branches the code
actually has. That shape came out of getting it wrong first: the initial version asserted
that every write documents 503, since the event stream can be down by design, and it
flagged uploadGlimtMediaHandler -- which was right, because uploading stores bytes in the
blob store and never publishes, so it has no 503 branch. The rule was wrong, not the
annotation. Comparing against the code cannot make that mistake.

It follows calls one level into other app methods, which is necessary rather than
thorough: requireGlimtModerator owns the 401/403/503 for all three moderation endpoints
and moderateGlimt owns hide and unhide entirely, so a walk stopping at the handler body
would have declared their documented failures unreachable. It checks both directions, and
the second is the one that earns its keep -- a documented failure the code cannot produce
is worse than a missing one and far harder to notice, because the spec then describes an
outcome that cannot happen and nothing looks wrong.

It found three real gaps on the first run, all the same shape: listGlimtHandler,
listGlimtHoldsHandler and devGlimtFixtureHandler all answer 404 when the caller has no
directory record, undocumented. That is reachable -- a signed-in member whose upstream row
is missing -- and a client reading the spec would have treated it as a bug to report. The
fixture handler was also missing the 400 it answers when a role maps to no group.

It also found one gap in itself: showGlimtMediaHandler's conditional-GET 304 is written
with w.WriteHeader rather than an error helper, so the walk now reads http.Status
constants passed to WriteHeader too, non-2xx only.

Scoped to Glimt on purpose. Widening it is a one-line change to isInScope and is worth
doing, but it would fail on handlers from other PRDs that nobody in this task touched, and
turning a guard red on unrelated work is how guards get commented out. Task 328 tracks
doing it properly.
